    {"ip": "10.30.6.41", "inferred_role": "marketing_workstation_infected", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T01:18:32+0100", "last_seen": "2027-11-18T01:23:18+0100", "agent_name": "WS-MKT-09.corp.local"},
    {"ip": "185.220.101.42", "inferred_role": "external_c2_exfil_endpoint", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T01:23:02+0100", "last_seen": "2027-11-18T01:23:18+0100"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Initial Access (Phishing Attachment)", "technique": "T1566.001", "what_happened": "At 2027-11-18 01:18:32 CET, Sysmon Event 1 on WS-MKT-09.corp.local (10.30.6.41) recorded winrar.exe opening an archive named setup_invoice.zip under user CORP\\jasmine.t. The archive name pattern (setup_*.zip / invoice_*.zip) is a classic phishing-attachment masquerade — the recipient is led to believe it contains an invoice document but the archive actually carries an executable payload. The 01:18 timing (just past midnight local) is also atypical for a marketing-team member's legitimate workflow.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image=*\\winrar.exe + commandLine contains 'setup_invoice.zip' + user=CORP\\jasmine.t. The user has no business unzipping an invoice archive at 01:18 — combined with the next-step payload drop, this is the unmistakable infection entry point.", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T01:18:32+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Execution / Defense Evasion (Unsigned Payload Drop)", "technique": "T1204.002 + T1027", "what_happened": "At 01:19:08 CET, Sysmon Event 11 recorded creation of C:\\Users\\jasmine.t\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\stealer.exe (signed=false, SHA256=c9d7e8f0a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8c9d0e1f2a3b4c5d6e7f8a9b0c1d2e3f4a5b6c7d8) — the binary extracted from the phishing zip. Dropping an UNSIGNED executable directly into the user's Temp directory is the canonical infostealer-loader pattern: avoids any need for elevation, blends with legitimate temp-file noise, and the file path is auto-cleaned by Windows. At 01:19:18, Sysmon Event 1 recorded stealer.exe launching with parentImage=explorer.exe (NOT winrar.exe directly — typical infostealer pattern where the operator double-clicks the extracted binary from the shell rather than the archive viewer).", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 11 file-create on the Temp\\stealer.exe path + Sysmon Event 1 process-create with image ending in stealer.exe + parentImage=explorer.exe + signed=false. The 36-second gap between unzip (01:18:32) and binary drop (01:19:08) plus another 10 seconds to launch (01:19:18) matches typical user-double-click + Defender-scan latency. Sysmon hash recorded for forensic correlation.", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T01:19:08+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Credential Access (Process Memory Read on Browser)", "technique": "T1555.003 + T1003", "what_happened": "At 01:22:47 CET, Sysmon Event 10 (ProcessAccess) fired: SourceImage=stealer.exe, TargetImage=chrome.exe, GrantedAccess=0x1000 (PROCESS_QUERY_LIMITED_INFORMATION / process memory read). The 0x1000 access mask is consistent with DPAPI master-key extraction from chrome.exe's process memory — the infostealer reads chrome.exe memory to harvest the decrypted DPAPI key used to unwrap Chrome's saved-credential AES-GCM encryption. This is the canonical 'browser credential theft via in-memory key extraction' pattern (replacing older approaches that decrypted Login Data offline using only the Local State JSON file).", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 10: sourceImage=*stealer.exe + targetImage=*chrome.exe + grantedAccess=0x1000. ProcessAccess from an unsigned Temp-directory binary to chrome.exe is anomalous for any legitimate workflow — even legitimate password managers don't read chrome.exe memory like this.", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T01:22:47+0100"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Credential Access (Chrome Login Data SQLite Read)", "technique": "T1555.003", "what_happened": "At 01:22:47 CET (same second as the ProcessAccess), Sysmon Event 11 recorded stealer.exe accessing C:\\Users\\jasmine.t\\AppData\\Local\\Google\\Chrome\\User Data\\Default\\Login Data — Chrome's SQLite database storing all saved website credentials (encrypted with AES-GCM under the DPAPI-wrapped master key extracted in step 3). At 01:22:52, the same process accessed C:\\Users\\jasmine.t\\AppData\\Local\\Google\\Chrome\\User Data\\Default\\Cookies — Chrome's SQLite database storing all session/auth cookies (worth as much as passwords for active SaaS sessions). At 01:22:55, the same process accessed C:\\Users\\jasmine.t\\AppData\\Local\\Google\\Chrome\\User Data\\Local State — JSON file containing the DPAPI-wrapped master encryption key. The 8-second sweep across the three Chrome credential artifacts (Login Data + Cookies + Local State) is the full infostealer harvest sequence: master key + credentials DB + cookies = complete browser credential takeover. EVERY website credential jasmine.t ever saved in Chrome on this workstation must be considered compromised. EVERY active SaaS session in any browser tab must be considered hijackable via cookie replay.", "chain_reason": "Three Sysmon Event 11 records on data.win.eventdata.image=*stealer.exe with targetFilename containing 'Chrome\\User Data\\Default\\Login Data', '...Cookies', '...Local State' within 8 seconds. The signature is universal: every infostealer (RedLine, Vidar, Raccoon, Lumma, StealC, Atomic) reads exactly these three artifacts in this order.", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T01:22:47+0100"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Command and Control / Exfiltration (Typosquatted SaaS-Look-Alike Domain)", "technique": "T1071.001 + T1041 + T1071.004", "what_happened": "At 01:23:02 CET, Sysmon Event 22 (DnsQuery) recorded stealer.exe querying sync-auth.cloud — a typosquatted SaaS-look-alike domain (.cloud TLD, name composition 'sync-auth' mimics legitimate identity-provider naming like 'auth.<saas>.com' or '<vendor>-auth.com'). The DNS resolved to 185.220.101.42. Sysmon Event 3 (NetworkConnect) on the same second recorded stealer.exe establishing an outbound TLS connection to 185.220.101.42 on port 443. The session transmitted ~3.2 MB POST to /api/upload — fully consistent with credential + cookie + Local State exfil payload size. The use of a SaaS-look-alike domain over standard HTTPS:443 is a deliberate detection-evasion choice — passes any 'is the protocol expected' / 'is the port expected' / 'does it look like cloud sync' surface heuristic. The detection signal is the DOMAIN ITSELF being typosquat-suspicious + the source process being an unsigned Temp-directory binary, NOT the port or protocol.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 22 queryName=sync-auth.cloud (typosquat heuristic), Sysmon Event 3 destinationIp=185.220.101.42 destinationPort=443 image=*stealer.exe, ~3.2MB transferred in single session. The image-source-Temp-directory + unsigned + first-seen-domain + cloud-look-alike-name + post-credential-harvest-timing combine into a high-confidence exfil signature even without payload-content inspection.", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T01:23:02+0100"}
  ],
